Dental Abscess/Trauma
Acute Urgent Care
Throbbing tooth pain, Tender to percussion, Fluctuant gingival swelling, Periapical radiolucency

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-026
Reason for Visit: Excruciating localized tooth pain with rapidly progressive facial swelling and inability to fully open the mouth.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient with severe, unrelenting dental pain, emerging facial cellulitis, and trismus (limited jaw opening) developing over the last 12 hours. Urgent face-to-face assessment was necessary to evaluate for deep space neck infection and impending airway compromise, as delaying until regular dental hours posed an unacceptable risk of systemic sepsis and fascial plane spread.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: This throbbing in my jaw is driving me insane, and now my cheek is ballooning up so much I can barely open my mouth.
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a 48-hour history of exquisite, localized, throbbing odontogenic pain in the right lower mandible. The pain started as a dull ache and sharply escalated over the past 12 hours. Concurrently, the patient developed unilateral facial swelling and severe trismus, limiting jaw opening to less than 2 centimeters. The patient reports a bad taste in the mouth and halitosis, alongside a subjective fever and general malaise. Pain is completely refractory to maximum doses of over-the-counter analgesics.
Functional Status: Pacing the room, holding an ice pack to the right cheek, unable to eat or drink normally.
Social/Safety Concerns: Impaired oral intake leading to mild dehydration.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 102 | Temp: 38.3 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Patient appears flushed, visibly distressed by pain, with gross asymmetry of the lower face.
Physical Exam: Maxillofacial examination reveals significant, warm, erythematous swelling over the right mandible extending into the submandibular space. Oral examination (limited by trismus) reveals an exquisitely tender right lower molar to light percussion. There is a fluctuant erythematous swelling of the adjacent gingiva with visible purulent exudate weeping from the gingival margin. The tooth crown feels slightly loose. Airway remains patent without deviation of the uvula or elevation of the tongue floor. Expected investigations would include an orthopantomogram (OPG) demonstrating a localized periapical radiolucency indicating bone loss.
Environmental Observations: Multiple empty boxes of ibuprofen and paracetamol on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular prescription medications. Exceeded recommended daily dose of NSAIDs.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Dental Abscess/Trauma - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Mumps/Parotitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of a localized fluctuant gingival mass, purulent dental exudate, and exquisite pain localized to a specific tooth upon percussion.

Trigeminal Neuralgia - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of a high fever, gross facial swelling, and continuous throbbing pain rather than brief, shock-like paroxysms triggered by light touch.

Peritonsillar Abscess - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of uvular deviation, a normal-sounding voice (no "hot potato" voice), and the clear odontogenic source of the swelling.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, severe dental abscess complicated by localized cellulitis and early trismus. The objective findings of a fluctuant gingival mass, purulent exudate, and facial swelling with a fever confirm an aggressive bacterial infection spreading beyond the periapical space. An urgent after-hours home visit was required to promptly institute high-dose systemic antibiotic therapy and robust analgesia, thereby arresting the spread of infection into the deep neck spaces (e.g., Ludwig's angina). Delaying this assessment until regular consulting hours would have exposed the patient to life-threatening airway compromise and systemic sepsis.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Intramuscular Ceftriaxone 1g and oral Metronidazole 400mg stat to target odontogenic flora. Provided oral Oxycodone 5mg for severe pain.
Positive Behavioral Change: By brushing twice daily and maintaining your routine bi-annual dental check-ups, you proactively remove the plaque that causes deep decay, permanently preventing these agonizing and dangerous infections.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent referral to an emergency dentist for extraction or root canal therapy within 12-24 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the swelling rapidly spreads down your neck, if you develop difficulty swallowing your own saliva, or if your breathing becomes noisy or restricted, call 000 immediately for an ambulance.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The antibiotics and painkillers will suppress the infection and pain temporarily, but the source of the infection remains inside the tooth.
Clinical Course: Without physical drainage of the abscess and treatment of the dead tooth by a dentist, the infection will inevitably return and worsen.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not apply warm compresses to the outside of your face, as this can draw the infection outward. Use cold packs for pain. Sleep with your head elevated on extra pillows to reduce the throbbing pressure.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: I have given you a strong antibiotic injection to immediately fight the infection spreading into your cheek, and a painkiller to help you sleep. The dentist must physically remove the trapped pus tomorrow.
Actionable Steps: Call your local dental clinic the moment they open to arrange an emergency extraction. Continue taking the prescribed oral antibiotics exactly on schedule to maintain a steady defense in your bloodstream.

